SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

The entitlements a confined plugin host is signed with when it has to load
plugins this project did not sign.

The sandbox is the same one the strict variant gets. What differs is library
validation: under a hardened runtime macOS loads a library only when it is signed
by Apple or by the same team as the host, so a plugin from another signer needs
`com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation`. That is a real weakening of
one of Apple's own layers, which is why it is opt-in, why it is a separate
bundle rather than a flag, and why the artifact verification the runtime already
performs — the kernel approves a digest, the host re-hashes what it received
before loading it — is the layer that remains when this one is off.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
